<h2>The case in one view</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Case</th><td>Suddhi v. The State NCT of Delhi, BAIL APPLN. 2025/2026</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Court</th><td>High Court of Delhi, Justice Mini Pushkarna</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Decided</th><td>3 November 2026</td></tr>
  <tr><th>FIR</th><td>No. 266/2023, PS Special Cell, Sections 20/25/29 NDPS Act</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Recovery alleged</th><td>About 32.230 kg charas — commercial quantity</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Outcome</th><td>Regular bail granted; arrest held illegal for non-supply of grounds of arrest</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The allegations</h2>
<p>The prosecution&rsquo;s case began with a secret informer&rsquo;s tip about an inter-state drug syndicate. On 19 October 2023, police intercepted a car at Gazipur, Delhi, and allegedly recovered fifteen packets of charas weighing about 32.230 kg from the petitioner. Disclosure statements then led to further arrests — one co-accused with about 29 kg of charas and cash, another later with a smaller quantity. The chargesheet was filed, and the trial before the Special Judge (NDPS), Patiala House Courts, stood at the stage of prosecution evidence when the bail application was heard.</p>

<h2>The decisive point: grounds of arrest</h2>
<p>The petitioner&rsquo;s principal ground was simple and, as it turned out, unanswerable: the grounds of his arrest were never supplied to him. When the matter was heard, the Additional Public Prosecutor conceded that no grounds of arrest had in fact been provided. The State instead took a legal position — that under <em>Mihir Rajesh Shah v. State of Maharashtra</em>, (2026) 1 SCC 500, the requirement of furnishing written grounds of arrest operates prospectively from 6 November 2025, and this arrest predated that cut-off.</p>
<p>The Court rejected the argument. <em>Mihir Rajesh Shah</em>, it reasoned, dealt with offences under the Indian Penal Code and the general procedure codes. The present case arose under the NDPS Act, a special statute, where the law on grounds of arrest had already been settled by <em>Pankaj Bansal</em> — decided on 3 October 2023, weeks before this arrest — and <em>Ram Kishor Arora</em>, (2024) 7 SCC 599. On that footing, the failure to furnish grounds was not excused by any question of prospectivity.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>The constitutional register:</strong> the Court observed that &ldquo;Article 22(1) of the Constitution is a substantive constitutional safeguard and not a procedural formality.&rdquo; An arrest made in breach of it is illegal — and the consequences follow regardless of the gravity of the recovery.</p>
</div>

<h2>Section 37 did not stand in the way</h2>
<p>Ordinarily, a commercial-quantity recovery triggers the twin conditions of Section 37 NDPS Act: the court must have reasonable grounds to believe the accused is not guilty and is not likely to offend on bail — a threshold that defeats most applications. Here, following the bail already granted on 21 January 2026 to a co-accused in the same FIR on the same ground, the Court held that Section 37 does not override the consequence of non-supply of grounds of arrest. Where the arrest itself is unconstitutional, the statutory rigour attached to the offence cannot cure it.</p>

<h2>Conditions of release</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>Personal bond of Rs 1,00,000 with one surety of the like amount.</li>
  <li>Monthly reporting to the IO/SHO, PS Special Cell, in the first week of each month.</li>
  <li>Appearance on every date of hearing; residence at the address in prison records, with prior intimation of any change.</li>
  <li>A working mobile number registered with the IO/SHO.</li>
  <li>No contact with, inducement to or threats against prosecution witnesses; no tampering with evidence; no fresh offence.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<p>The Court clarified, as is standard, that its observations were confined to the bail application and expressed no opinion on the merits of the trial.</p>

<h2>Why the decision matters</h2>
<p>The judgment adds to a consistent line of Delhi High Court authority treating the supply of grounds of arrest as a jurisdictional fact of a lawful arrest, with release as the remedy for breach — even in commercial-quantity NDPS cases. Two practical lessons stand out. For the defence, the arrest memo and accompanying documents should be scrutinised in every NDPS matter at the earliest stage: a missing or defective communication of grounds can change the complexion of a case otherwise governed by Section 37. For investigating agencies, the ruling is a reminder that the special statute offers no exemption from Article 22(1), and that a defect at the moment of arrest cannot be repaired by the weight of the recovery. The parity principle also did real work here: once a co-accused secured bail on the identical ground, the same consequence followed for the petitioner.</p>
